1
00:00:01,140 --> 00:00:03,468
So we learned about function declarations

2
00:00:03,468 --> 00:00:06,260
and expressions in the last video

3
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:08,320
but there is actually a third type

4
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:12,150
of function that was added to JavaScript in ES6

5
00:00:12,150 --> 00:00:13,963
and that's the arrow function.

6
00:00:15,210 --> 00:00:18,380
And an arrow function is simply a special form

7
00:00:18,380 --> 00:00:21,170
of function expression that is shorter

8
00:00:21,170 --> 00:00:23,900
and therefore faster to write.

9
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:26,950
So let's actually get back this function expression

10
00:00:26,950 --> 00:00:28,490
from before here.

11
00:00:28,490 --> 00:00:30,120
So from the previous lecture

12
00:00:30,120 --> 00:00:33,040
just so that we have a comparison.

13
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:34,753
So like a reference point.

14
00:00:36,390 --> 00:00:40,793
And so now let's write the arrow function.

15
00:00:41,650 --> 00:00:43,933
And this is how we write an arrow function.

16
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:47,400
So again, we want the birthYeah

17
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:50,220
because we want to calculate the age again

18
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:52,950
and then we write an arrow

19
00:00:52,950 --> 00:00:54,530
and that's the reason why this function

20
00:00:54,530 --> 00:00:56,480
is called arrow function.

21
00:00:56,480 --> 00:00:59,810
And then we simply write what we want to return.

22
00:00:59,810 --> 00:01:01,130
So in this case,

23
00:01:01,130 --> 00:01:03,613
that's 2037

24
00:01:03,613 --> 00:01:07,940
minus the birthYeah and that's it.

25
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:11,220
Now, to finish, we actually want

26
00:01:11,220 --> 00:01:13,630
to be able to use this function.

27
00:01:13,630 --> 00:01:16,630
So let's again store it in a variable,

28
00:01:16,630 --> 00:01:19,653
just like we did here with this function expression.

29
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:26,560
So let's say const calcAge3 this time

30
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:29,510
and then we set that variable

31
00:01:29,510 --> 00:01:31,981
to this function here, okay?

32
00:01:31,981 --> 00:01:34,910
So as I said, this is a special form

33
00:01:34,910 --> 00:01:36,380
of a function expression

34
00:01:36,380 --> 00:01:39,860
because it still is a function expression actually.

35
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:42,860
So it's a value, all of this,

36
00:01:42,860 --> 00:01:44,930
that we assign to this variable.

37
00:01:44,930 --> 00:01:48,000
So just like this function expression up here.

38
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:49,300
But this one, as you see,

39
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:52,430
is a lot easier and faster to write.

40
00:01:52,430 --> 00:01:53,730
The first reason for that

41
00:01:53,730 --> 00:01:56,085
is that we don't need the curly braces,

42
00:01:56,085 --> 00:01:59,090
like we have here, to define a code block.

43
00:01:59,090 --> 00:02:03,810
And second is that the return actually happens implicitly.

44
00:02:03,810 --> 00:02:07,230
So this value here will automatically be returned

45
00:02:07,230 --> 00:02:12,050
without us having to explicitly write the return keyword.

46
00:02:12,050 --> 00:02:12,920
Okay?

47
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:16,280
So this is excellent for simple one-liner functions.

48
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:17,990
And you will see throughout the course

49
00:02:17,990 --> 00:02:20,400
that this is gonna be extremely helpful

50
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:21,923
in certain situations.

51
00:02:23,150 --> 00:02:25,000
And now to use this function,

52
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,220
it, of course, works the exact same way

53
00:02:27,220 --> 00:02:29,380
as using all the other functions.

54
00:02:29,380 --> 00:02:31,440
So we call calcAge3

55
00:02:32,570 --> 00:02:37,150
just like we called the other calcAge functions.

56
00:02:37,150 --> 00:02:38,730
Okay.

57
00:02:38,730 --> 00:02:43,730
And again, we save the returned value here to a variable

58
00:02:45,485 --> 00:02:49,633
and then let's just check it out here.

59
00:02:51,870 --> 00:02:54,160
And so this one should be 46 again,

60
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:56,210
just like we had in the previous lecture.

61
00:02:57,090 --> 00:02:59,493
And yeah, it works.

62
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:02,900
So indeed, the value that we calculated

63
00:03:02,900 --> 00:03:05,740
was returned automatically without us having

64
00:03:05,740 --> 00:03:07,950
to write the return keyword.

65
00:03:07,950 --> 00:03:10,940
Now, you see that here we also didn't need any parentheses

66
00:03:10,940 --> 00:03:12,530
or anything like that.

67
00:03:12,530 --> 00:03:14,893
Just this here is the function.

68
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:16,793
Okay?

69
00:03:17,875 --> 00:03:19,630
Now, this is actually just the simplest form,

70
00:03:19,630 --> 00:03:22,761
which is when we only have exactly one parameter

71
00:03:22,761 --> 00:03:26,050
and only basically one line of code

72
00:03:26,050 --> 00:03:28,430
in which we want to return something.

73
00:03:28,430 --> 00:03:30,540
But it gets a little bit more complex

74
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:34,350
when we add more parameters or more code.

75
00:03:34,350 --> 00:03:36,780
So let's now write another function

76
00:03:37,620 --> 00:03:39,630
and we can get rid of this one

77
00:03:39,630 --> 00:03:42,040
since it was just a copy anyway.

78
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:44,750
And so let's say that we want to calculate

79
00:03:44,750 --> 00:03:49,050
how many years a person has left until retirement.

80
00:03:49,050 --> 00:03:52,413
So let's call this function yearsUntilRetirement.

81
00:03:56,790 --> 00:03:59,600
And then we set that equal to a function.

82
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:02,547
And once again, let's pass in the birthYeah.

83
00:04:04,730 --> 00:04:06,100
Then the arrow again

84
00:04:06,100 --> 00:04:08,400
but now, here we need some more code

85
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:10,550
because to calculate the number of years

86
00:04:10,550 --> 00:04:14,250
until the retirement based on the birthYeah alone,

87
00:04:14,250 --> 00:04:16,290
we first need to calculate the age,

88
00:04:16,290 --> 00:04:19,760
and then from there, we need to calculate the retirement age

89
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:21,423
minus the current age.

90
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:24,700
So we need more lines of code

91
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:28,910
and so we are back to actually needing the curly braces

92
00:04:28,910 --> 00:04:32,270
in order to define a code block like this.

93
00:04:32,270 --> 00:04:34,260
So let's do what we just said.

94
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:37,230
So calculate the age based on the birth year.

95
00:04:37,230 --> 00:04:41,790
So 2037 minus the birthYeah

96
00:04:42,690 --> 00:04:45,060
and now let's calculate

97
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:49,003
the years until retirement basically.

98
00:04:49,890 --> 00:04:53,975
And so let's say that the retirement age is 65 years,

99
00:04:53,975 --> 00:04:57,963
as it is in many countries here in Europe at least.

100
00:04:58,890 --> 00:05:02,430
So 65 and then minus the current age.

101
00:05:02,430 --> 00:05:06,090
And that will then give us how many years we have left.

102
00:05:06,090 --> 00:05:08,320
And now to return this retirement,

103
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:13,320
we actually need to write the return keyword explicitly.

104
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:17,460
So we can only omit the return here

105
00:05:17,460 --> 00:05:21,320
in case we have a one-liner function like here.

106
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:22,540
But if we have more,

107
00:05:22,540 --> 00:05:25,293
then we need to do the return still.

108
00:05:26,149 --> 00:05:29,540
So let's now run this function.

109
00:05:29,540 --> 00:05:32,230
So yearsUntilRetirement

110
00:05:32,230 --> 00:05:33,950
and then again some year

111
00:05:34,970 --> 00:05:39,030
and then as always, we could capture the return value

112
00:05:39,030 --> 00:05:40,600
into a variable.

113
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:43,620
But this time, let's actually log that value

114
00:05:43,620 --> 00:05:45,003
to the console directly.

115
00:05:46,010 --> 00:05:48,370
So console.log

116
00:05:50,020 --> 00:05:53,093
and yeah, so let's try that.

117
00:05:54,140 --> 00:05:55,593
And so it's 19.

118
00:05:56,735 --> 00:06:00,400
And that makes sense because 65 minus 46,

119
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:03,800
which is the age, is 19.

120
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:04,820
Okay?

121
00:06:04,820 --> 00:06:08,010
So that's the scenario when we have one parameter here

122
00:06:08,010 --> 00:06:11,670
and then more than one line of code basically.

123
00:06:11,670 --> 00:06:13,210
And remember, in that situation,

124
00:06:13,210 --> 00:06:16,220
we need the return statement here.

125
00:06:16,220 --> 00:06:19,760
But now, what if we had multiple parameters?

126
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:20,810
Well, then we need

127
00:06:20,810 --> 00:06:24,290
to wrap the parameters here into parentheses.

128
00:06:24,290 --> 00:06:26,980
So in VS Code, we can just select a variable

129
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:29,290
and then write parentheses

130
00:06:29,290 --> 00:06:32,400
and then it will automatically wrap this variable

131
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:35,620
into parentheses on both sides.

132
00:06:35,620 --> 00:06:37,560
So let's say we want the birthYeah

133
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:39,960
but also the firstName

134
00:06:39,960 --> 00:06:42,910
so that we can like return something else,

135
00:06:42,910 --> 00:06:44,260
like a sentence.

136
00:06:44,260 --> 00:06:46,800
So let's comment out this part here

137
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:49,830
and instead, let's return a string.

138
00:06:49,830 --> 00:06:52,070
And again, we're using a template string here

139
00:06:52,070 --> 00:06:53,830
to build that string

140
00:06:53,830 --> 00:06:57,600
and basically, I want to say like firstName retires

141
00:06:57,600 --> 00:06:59,760
in x years.

142
00:06:59,760 --> 00:07:02,783
So let's use that placeholder here.

143
00:07:03,830 --> 00:07:08,830
So firstName retires in

144
00:07:09,080 --> 00:07:11,663
and then we use that value that we just calculated.

145
00:07:12,740 --> 00:07:14,023
So retirement.

146
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:17,210
Give it a save here

147
00:07:17,210 --> 00:07:18,950
and now we need to call it

148
00:07:18,950 --> 00:07:22,540
and so let's add the second argument here

149
00:07:22,540 --> 00:07:25,500
so that it will go to the second parameter.

150
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:27,900
So the first one here is, of course, birthYeah

151
00:07:27,900 --> 00:07:30,660
and so that corresponds to 1991

152
00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:34,080
and now the second argument will go to the second parameter,

153
00:07:34,080 --> 00:07:35,230
which is the firstName.

154
00:07:36,350 --> 00:07:38,360
So let's say Jonas

155
00:07:38,360 --> 00:07:40,020
and let's just call it twice

156
00:07:41,110 --> 00:07:42,830
so that it actually makes more sense

157
00:07:42,830 --> 00:07:46,770
that we even put this logic into its own function.

158
00:07:46,770 --> 00:07:51,110
So let's say that Bob was born in 1980

159
00:07:52,420 --> 00:07:54,793
and so let's see how many years he has left.

160
00:07:58,010 --> 00:08:00,220
So Jonas retires in 19 years,

161
00:08:00,220 --> 00:08:03,300
Bob retires in eight years.

162
00:08:03,300 --> 00:08:07,980
Great, and that's essentially how the arrow functions work.

163
00:08:08,983 --> 00:08:10,450
You just need to keep in mind the couple

164
00:08:10,450 --> 00:08:13,320
of different scenarios that there are regarding

165
00:08:13,320 --> 00:08:14,920
to the number of lines of code

166
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:18,010
that you need and the number of parameters.

167
00:08:18,010 --> 00:08:19,550
So as you add more code

168
00:08:19,550 --> 00:08:22,830
and more parameters, it gets a little bit more complex

169
00:08:22,830 --> 00:08:24,420
and we kind of lose the advantage

170
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:26,560
of using an arrow function.

171
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:30,320
So now you might ask the same question as before.

172
00:08:30,320 --> 00:08:33,130
What type of function should I use?

173
00:08:33,130 --> 00:08:35,493
Should I use arrow functions for everything

174
00:08:35,493 --> 00:08:37,880
since they're so easy to write?

175
00:08:37,880 --> 00:08:40,270
And well, the answer is no.

176
00:08:40,270 --> 00:08:42,484
But it's also complicated.

177
00:08:42,484 --> 00:08:45,440
That's because there is another fundamental difference

178
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:49,150
between the arrow function and more traditional functions.

179
00:08:49,150 --> 00:08:52,620
So function declarations and function expressions.

180
00:08:52,620 --> 00:08:54,520
It's the fact that arrow function

181
00:08:54,520 --> 00:08:57,750
do not get a so-called this keyword.

182
00:08:57,750 --> 00:09:01,550
And this, once more, is a topic for much later.

183
00:09:01,550 --> 00:09:05,495
So remember that learning is not at all a linear process

184
00:09:05,495 --> 00:09:09,100
and so topics need to build up on one another

185
00:09:09,100 --> 00:09:10,750
in an incremental way.

186
00:09:10,750 --> 00:09:13,280
Otherwise, you would just get super overwhelmed

187
00:09:13,280 --> 00:09:16,490
and super stressed if I were to tell you everything

188
00:09:16,490 --> 00:09:17,483
at the same time.

189
00:09:17,483 --> 00:09:19,894
So for now, we will actually keep

190
00:09:19,894 --> 00:09:22,900
using mainly the normal functions,

191
00:09:22,900 --> 00:09:26,180
except for very simple one-liner functions.

192
00:09:26,180 --> 00:09:28,663
For example, like this one here.

193
00:09:28,663 --> 00:09:31,100
And that doesn't mean that arrow functions

194
00:09:31,100 --> 00:09:32,400
are not important.

195
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:33,790
They absolutely are

196
00:09:33,790 --> 00:09:36,696
and I love them and I use them all the time.

197
00:09:36,696 --> 00:09:40,090
Sometimes I don't write regular functions at all.

198
00:09:40,090 --> 00:09:43,280
But again, you cannot yet understand all the implications

199
00:09:43,280 --> 00:09:45,240
of using arrow functions.

200
00:09:45,240 --> 00:09:47,036
So for now again, we will keep using

201
00:09:47,036 --> 00:09:50,423
probably the function expressions the most.